1. **A method for determining left and right eigenvector phases of a vibrating flow tube, the method comprising:**

   applying, by a first driver coupled to a first side of the vibrating flow tube, a first excitation signal having a first carrier frequency offset from a resonance frequency of the vibrating flow tube and amplitude-modulated by a modulation signal;

   applying, by a second driver coupled to a second side of the vibrating flow tube, a second excitation signal having a second carrier frequency offset from the resonance frequency, wherein an amplitude modulation of the second excitation signal is out of phase with the modulation signal of the first excitation signal;

   receiving, from first and second pickoffs disposed at respective locations along the vibrating flow tube, first and second pickoff signals representing vibration of the vibrating flow tube responsive to the first and second excitation signals;

   demodulating the first and second pickoff signals with reference to the first and second excitation signals to obtain complex vibration-response values at the first and second carrier frequencies;

   generating, from the complex vibration-response values, a complex response matrix relating excitation by the first and second drivers to vibration detected by the first and second pickoffs;

   determining a right eigenvector and a left eigenvector from the complex response matrix; and

   determining a right eigenvector phase from a phase relationship between components of the right eigenvector and a left eigenvector phase from a phase relationship between components of the left eigenvector.

**Example Independent Method Claim**

1. A method for characterizing a vibratory flow tube, the method comprising:  
   exciting a vibration mode of the flow tube using first and second actuators;  
   driving the first and second actuators with respective amplitude-modulated drive signals having mutually out-of-phase modulation components, the drive signals comprising \(N+1\) mutually independent excitation signals;  
   applying, through the first and second actuators, a plurality of excitation frequencies offset from a resonant frequency of the vibration mode;  
   determining, from responses of the flow tube at the plurality of excitation frequencies, a phase relationship between at least one of the first and second actuators and the vibration mode;  
   extrapolating, based on the determined phase relationship, a phase of a left eigenvector of the vibration mode at the resonant frequency;  
   receiving respective response signals from a left pickoff and a right pickoff coupled to the flow tube; and  
   determining, based on the respective response signals, a phase of a right eigenvector of the vibration mode.

**Specification-Style Description**

A vibration mode of a flow tube is excited by first and second actuators. The actuators are driven using amplitude-modulated drive signals whose modulation components are out of phase with one another and which collectively include \(N+1\) independent excitation signals. The excitation signals are applied at a plurality of frequencies displaced from the resonant frequency of the vibration mode. Measured responses at the displaced frequencies are used to infer a driver-to-mode phase relationship. The inferred phase relationship is extrapolated to the resonant frequency to determine the phase of the left eigenvector at resonance. Response signals obtained from left and right pickoffs are further used to determine the phase of the right eigenvector of the vibration mode.
